  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "OLiVES: An Outdoor Low-Light Video Benchmark for Enhancement and Segmentation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20501",
      "description": "arXiv:2610.20501v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Low-light video processing remains an underexplored problem in computer vision, primarily due to the scarcity of high-quality ground-truth data. Existing public datasets exhibit several limitations, including restricted motion diversity, limited scene coverage, weak degradations, reliance on auxiliary modalities such as event data, and misalignment between low-light inputs and ground truth. Furthermore, current datasets typically address either low-light enhancement or scene understanding, whereas these tasks should be considered jointly. We introduce \\textbf{OLiVES}, a benchmark dataset for outdoor low-light videos with dynamic scenes, designed to support both low-light video enhancement (LLVE) and video object segmentation (VOS). The dataset addresses key content gaps by incorporating a diverse range of natural textures, animals, occlusions, dynamic activities, and close-up scenes. To the best of our knowledge, OLiVES is the first outdoor low-light video dataset captured using professional cinema-grade cameras. It provides over 25,000 aligned normal- and low-light frame pairs, along with over 46,000 VOS annotations. We benchmark state-of-the-art LLVE and VOS methods on OLiVES and show that training on it improves performance on both tasks. The OLiVES project page is available at: https://anonymous-OLiVES.github.io/.",
      "source_name": "arXiv Computer Vision",
      "published": "2026-10-15T04:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "ELVIS: Enhance Low-Light for Video Instance Segmentation in the Dark",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.01495",
      "description": "arXiv:2512.01495v4 Announce Type: replace \nAbstract: Video instance segmentation (VIS) for low-light content remains highly challenging for both humans and machines alike, due to noise, blur and other adverse conditions. The lack of large-scale annotated datasets and the limitations of current synthetic pipelines, particularly in modeling temporal degradations, further hinder progress. Moreover, existing VIS methods are not robust to the degradations found in low-light videos and, consequently, perform poorly even after finetuning. In this paper, we introduce \\textbf{ELVIS} (\\textbf{E}nhance \\textbf{L}ow-Light for \\textbf{V}ideo \\textbf{I}nstance \\textbf{S}egmentation), a framework that enables domain adaptation of state-of-the-art VIS models to low-light scenarios. ELVIS is comprised of an unsupervised synthetic low-light video pipeline that models both spatial and temporal degradations, a calibration-free degradation profile estimation network (VDP-Net) and an enhancement decoder head that disentangles degradations from content features. ELVIS improves performances by up to \\textbf{+3.7AP} on the synthetic low-light YouTube-VIS 2019 dataset and beats two-stage baselines by at least \\textbf{+2.8AP} on real low-light videos. Code and dataset available at: \\href{https://joannelin168.github.io/research/ELVIS}{https://joannelin168.github.io/research/ELVIS}",
      "source_name": "arXiv Computer Vision",
      "published": "2026-10-15T04:00:00"
    }
  ]
